\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8}The obligation \& enforcement organ: the sovereign's two arms}{16}{section.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:enforcement}{{8}{16}{The obligation \& enforcement organ: the sovereign's two arms}{section.8}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces The kernel's deontic split (Jones \& Sergot), the formal core of its obligation/enforcement organ: \emph  {prohibition} is regimented \emph  {or} detected, \emph  {obligation} is enforced by oracle-bound commitments, \emph  {permission} is a recorded capability --- and the coordination layer's deontic binding is a direct lift of this split. The regimented set (double-claim, duplicate-identity squat, serve-from-test-database, refuse-to-serve on a failed self-check) is rejected pre-commit by a uniqueness constraint or fail-closed boot gate; everything the policy monitor \emph  {subscribes} to (heartbeat freshness, note monotonicity, capability escalation, lock-owner validity) is detected post-commit and compensated. Commitments follow Cohen \& Levesque's persistent goals: open $\to $ done $\mid $ abandoned $\mid $ superseded, with Law~1 closing the Goodhart hole on deadlines and Law~2 refusing free-text closure. And capability (ability) is kept distinct from permission (normative status) so that ``able but forbidden'' --- e.g.\ a dangerous override flag that \emph  {exists} but \emph  {must not be used} --- remains representable, the very state a guardrail-bypass discipline depends on.}}{16}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:swk-deontic-split}{{7}{16}{The kernel's deontic split (Jones \& Sergot), the formal core of its obligation/enforcement organ: \emph {prohibition} is regimented \emph {or} detected, \emph {obligation} is enforced by oracle-bound commitments, \emph {permission} is a recorded capability --- and the coordination layer's deontic binding is a direct lift of this split. The regimented set (double-claim, duplicate-identity squat, serve-from-test-database, refuse-to-serve on a failed self-check) is rejected pre-commit by a uniqueness constraint or fail-closed boot gate; everything the policy monitor \emph {subscribes} to (heartbeat freshness, note monotonicity, capability escalation, lock-owner validity) is detected post-commit and compensated. Commitments follow Cohen \& Levesque's persistent goals: open $\to $ done $\mid $ abandoned $\mid $ superseded, with Law~1 closing the Goodhart hole on deadlines and Law~2 refusing free-text closure. And capability (ability) is kept distinct from permission (normative status) so that ``able but forbidden'' --- e.g.\ a dangerous override flag that \emph {exists} but \emph {must not be used} --- remains representable, the very state a guardrail-bypass discipline depends on}{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.1}The deontic split}{16}{subsection.8.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{def:deontic}{{8.1}{16}{The kernel's deontic split}{definition.8.1}{}}
