\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.2}The policy monitor is a detector, not a regimenter}{17}{subsection.8.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:arbiter-correction}{{8.2}{17}{The policy monitor is a detector, not a regimenter}{subsection.8.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces The kernel as a reference monitor --- and the honest correction it forces. Anderson's reference monitor must be \emph  {always invoked} on the request path (complete mediation), \emph  {tamper-evident}, and \emph  {small enough to verify}. The \textbf  {uniqueness constraint / boot gate} (cobalt) meets the first property by construction --- the single SQLite file lock means every double-claim, duplicate-identity squat, or serve-from-test-database attempt is rejected \emph  {before} the WAL write. The \textbf  {policy monitor} (teal) does not: it is a subscriber to the operation log that fires \emph  {after} the event is already durable, and by Schneider's theorem~\cite  {schneider2000} (\S  \ref {sec:enforcement}) a monitor downstream of the commit line enforces no safety property --- it can only \emph  {detect and compensate} within a bounded window. Tamper-evidence (a hash-chained audit log) is re-scoped to the economy layer (\S  \ref {sec:merkle}); smallness is the point of one writer over one file. We therefore reserve ``physically unreachable'' for the regimented set and say ``detected, then halted or compensated'' everywhere else.}}{17}{figure.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:swk-reference-monitor}{{8}{17}{The kernel as a reference monitor --- and the honest correction it forces. Anderson's reference monitor must be \emph {always invoked} on the request path (complete mediation), \emph {tamper-evident}, and \emph {small enough to verify}. The \textbf {uniqueness constraint / boot gate} (cobalt) meets the first property by construction --- the single SQLite file lock means every double-claim, duplicate-identity squat, or serve-from-test-database attempt is rejected \emph {before} the WAL write. The \textbf {policy monitor} (teal) does not: it is a subscriber to the operation log that fires \emph {after} the event is already durable, and by Schneider's theorem~\cite {schneider2000} (\S \ref {sec:enforcement}) a monitor downstream of the commit line enforces no safety property --- it can only \emph {detect and compensate} within a bounded window. Tamper-evidence (a hash-chained audit log) is re-scoped to the economy layer (\S \ref {sec:merkle}); smallness is the point of one writer over one file. We therefore reserve ``physically unreachable'' for the regimented set and say ``detected, then halted or compensated'' everywhere else}{figure.caption.14}{}}
\newlabel{thm:schneider}{{8.1}{17}{A monitoring bound on post-commit enforcement}{theorem.8.1}{}}
